from . import T
T('9-layers-switching','layers','Principles of Layering','Layering, encapsulation and addresses at each hop',r'''
Layering divides communication responsibilities into interfaces. An upper layer uses a lower layer’s service without exposing every implementation detail; peer protocols describe behavior between corresponding layers across hosts. Application messages can be carried in transport segments, network packets and link frames, each adding its own control information.
A router forwards network packets between links and normally constructs new link framing for the next hop. Link-layer destination identifies a neighbor on that link, while the packet’s network destination generally identifies the remote host. Transport ports distinguish application endpoints at a host. These address roles should not be merged into one undifferentiated “destination.”
Encapsulation adds headers and sometimes trailers, so payload size at one layer differs from total size at the next. MTU is a link/network packet limit under the stated context, not automatically the application-message limit. GATE traces should identify which devices inspect which headers and when addresses change. Layering is a design abstraction, so protocol-specific exceptions such as tunneling or NAT need their stated rules.
''','A host sends to a remote host via a router on the local Ethernet.',r'Network destination::::The IP packet names the remote destination host.~Local link destination::::The first frame names the router’s local interface, obtained through the appropriate neighbor-resolution mechanism.~Router forwarding::::The router chooses the next hop and builds a frame for its outgoing link.', 'First-hop link destination is the router, while IP destination is the remote host.','The remote host’s link address is not directly used on an unrelated local Ethernet.')
T('9-layers-switching','switching','Circuit switching;Packet switching;Virtual circuit','Circuit, datagram and virtual-circuit switching',r'''
Circuit switching establishes a communication path with reserved resources for a session, paying setup before transfer and possibly wasting reservation during idle periods. Packet switching divides data into packets sharing link capacity statistically; queues and variable delay can arise under contention. A datagram service forwards packets independently using destination routing information.
A virtual circuit is a connection-oriented packet-switched arrangement: setup establishes state and a path/label relationship, after which packets follow that connection’s forwarding context. It is not automatically a dedicated physical circuit with guaranteed bandwidth. Labels can be locally meaningful and change between hops.
Compare setup delay, per-packet header/state, utilization and failure behavior using the model given. Statistical sharing can improve average utilization without guaranteeing zero delay. Store-and-forward means an entire packet is received before forwarding, while other switching implementations can have different timing. GATE questions often distinguish reserved resources from merely maintaining a logical connection.
''','A virtual circuit is established through routers A,B,C. Does this prove an exclusive link reservation?',r'Connection state::::Setup installs forwarding information for the logical path.~Packet sharing::::Other circuits/datagrams may still share the same physical links under the service model.~Guarantee check::::A reservation or QoS contract must be explicitly supplied to infer exclusive capacity.', 'A virtual circuit alone does not prove dedicated bandwidth.','Logical path establishment and physical resource reservation are distinct concepts.')
T('9-layers-switching','delays','Performance metrics','Transmission, propagation, throughput and pipeline timing',r'''
Transmission delay is L/R for L bits at R bits per second; it is the time to put the packet onto a link. Propagation delay is distance divided by signal speed and concerns travel after a bit is launched. Queuing waits for service and processing covers local handling. End-to-end delay sums relevant path events, accounting for overlap where the model permits it.
Throughput is delivered data per time and is limited by bottlenecks, while latency is one item’s elapsed time. A bandwidth-delay product estimates bits in flight during a specified propagation/round-trip interval. Unit conversion matters: packet bytes need multiplying by eight when rates use bits/s.
Across equal-rate store-and-forward links, the first packet pays transmission on every link, while later packets can pipeline across them. Do not multiply single-packet end-to-end serialization by packet count when transfers overlap. Queue delay requires supplied load or a model; it cannot be deduced from length and rate alone. Keep one-way delay separate from RTT.
''','Two equal links, each taking t to serialize one packet, send three packets with no other delays.',r'First packet::::It exits link one at t and link two at 2t.~Overlap::::Link one sends packet two while link two sends packet one.~Final completion::T=(N+1)t=4t::Packets two and three then exit at 3t and 4t.', 'Total 4t, not 6t.','Three transmissions occur on each link, but their timelines overlap across different links.')
T('9-link-layer','framing','Data link layer;Error detection','Frames and error-detection capabilities',r'''
The data-link layer delivers frames across one link, defining framing boundaries, link addressing and error-detection/control behavior. A receiver must identify a complete frame despite an underlying stream of bits; schemes may use lengths, delimiter patterns and escaping/bit stuffing. Link reliability is distinct from end-to-end transport reliability.
Parity adds a bit so the protected word has prescribed parity. It detects any odd number of bit flips but can miss even counts and does not identify the corrupted position alone. Checksums combine words under a specified arithmetic rule; CRC uses polynomial divisibility. A detector can fail for some structured error patterns, so “redundant bits added” does not imply perfect detection or correction.
Retransmission protocols use sequence numbers and acknowledgments to recover detected losses/errors, with stop-and-wait or sliding-window behavior. Detection, correction and medium sharing are different objectives. GATE problems should state the code/generator and error model before claiming a guarantee. Include framing/control overhead when calculating useful payload utilization if total wire bytes are given.
''','Even parity protects data 1011. Add parity and consider one or two bit flips.',r'Parity bit::::Data has three ones, so append 1 to make four ones.~One flip::::The total parity becomes odd and the error is detected.~Two flips::::Parity can remain even despite corrupted data, so detection is not guaranteed.', 'Parity detects single flips but does not guarantee detection of arbitrary multi-bit errors.','The parity mismatch cannot by itself locate which bit changed, so it is not a general correction scheme.')
T('9-link-layer','crc','CRC','CRC polynomial division without carries',r'''
CRC interprets a bit string as a polynomial over GF(2), where addition/subtraction are XOR. A generator of degree r requires r check bits. Append r zero bits to the data, divide by the generator using aligned XOR, and attach the resulting r-bit remainder to the original data. The transmitted codeword then divides with zero remainder.
At reception, a nonzero remainder detects inconsistency. An error whose polynomial is divisible by the generator can evade detection, so exact guarantees depend on generator factors and error length. The conventional nonzero constant term ensures relevant trailing behavior. CRC is normally detection, not automatic correction.
Write each XOR alignment rather than using decimal integer division or subtraction with borrows. A generator’s bit length is r+1, while remainder width is r. Keep leading zeros in that remainder to preserve field width. GATE examples may ask the codeword, remainder or whether a supplied error pattern is detected; these are related but not identical tasks.
''','Data 1001, generator 1011. Compute the codeword.',r'Append zeros::1001\to1001000::Generator degree is three.~XOR division::::Aligned divisions leave remainder 110, without arithmetic borrow.~Transmit::codeword=1001110::Replace appended zeros by the remainder.', '1001110.','Dividing 1001110 by 1011 using XOR gives remainder zero, verifying construction.')
T('9-link-layer','ethernet','Medium Access Control;Ethernet','Shared-medium access and Ethernet duplex assumptions',r'''
Medium access control coordinates transmitters sharing a channel. ALOHA transmits with limited coordination and retries collisions; carrier-sense methods first inspect activity. Classic half-duplex shared Ethernet uses CSMA/CD, detecting collisions while sending and backing off. The minimum frame-time reasoning ensures distant collisions can still be observed before transmission ends.
Modern switched full-duplex Ethernet separates transmit/receive paths and does not use classic collision detection on those links. A switch learns source link addresses and forwards frames according to its table, flooding when a destination is unknown under ordinary behavior. This is different from an IP router’s longest-prefix decision.
State topology, duplex and link rate before applying a shared-bus formula. Wireless collision avoidance uses different assumptions from collision detection, since hearing the channel does not guarantee the receiver sees no interference. GATE distinctions often test whether CSMA/CD applies to every Ethernet situation. Link-layer addressing and frame checks still exist in full duplex even though shared-medium collisions do not.
''','A full-duplex point-to-point link connects a host and Ethernet switch.',r'Channel structure::::Sending and receiving do not contend for one shared half-duplex collision domain.~Collision protocol::::Classic CSMA/CD is not used for that full-duplex link.~Other link duties::::Framing, link addresses and error checks remain needed.', 'Full-duplex switched Ethernet does not require classic collision detection.','Removing collision detection does not remove the data-link layer or turn the switch into an IP router.')
T('9-routing','dv','Distance vector routing;Count to infinity','Distance-vector updates and stale-route feedback',r'''
A distance-vector router learns destination estimates from neighbors. For destination y, compare local link cost to neighbor v plus that neighbor’s advertised remaining distance and choose the minimum. The router need not know the complete topology. Advertisements can be stale and propagate changes gradually.
Count-to-infinity arises when routers incorrectly treat each other’s old routes as independent ways to reach a failed destination. A’s route may depend on B while B’s depends on A, producing a loop and increasing metrics. Split horizon suppresses advertising a learned route back along its incoming interface; poison reverse reports it as unreachable there. These mitigate specific feedback patterns, not every possible convergence issue.
Trace next hops as well as numeric values to expose circular dependencies. A cheaper first link does not necessarily produce the cheapest end-to-end route. Use the stated infinity bound and update schedule if an exact iteration count is requested. Distance-vector logic applies a Bellman minimum; it should not be confused with a link-state algorithm that reconstructs a full graph.
''','Neighbor A costs 2 and advertises 5 to D; B costs 4 and advertises 1.',r'Via A::candidate_A=2+5=7::Include the local hop.~Via B::candidate_B=4+1=5::Its farther first hop has a cheaper suffix.~Choose::D(D)=\min(7,5)=5::Use B as next hop.', 'Choose B with total advertised cost 5.','Comparing only link costs 2 and 4 would wrongly select A.')
T('9-routing','ls','Link state routing','Link-state flooding and shortest-path computation',r'''
Link-state routing advertises each router’s local links and costs so participating routers can assemble a topology database. Flooding distributes these descriptions, with sequence/age mechanisms helping distinguish new information from old. Each router then computes shortest paths from itself, commonly using Dijkstra when costs are nonnegative.
Unlike distance vector, a router computes from a graph rather than simply adding neighbor distance advertisements. Each source can derive a different shortest-path tree from the same topology. Consistent databases favor consistent routing, but propagation delay and old snapshots can create transient effects, so “global topology” does not mean instantaneous perfect convergence.
For a routing-table exercise, first construct the graph from supplied link states, then calculate paths and extract next hops. A path cost and its next hop are different outputs: the destination may be several links away while the table names only the first neighbor. Tie-breaking can select among equal-cost paths. Do not count flooding messages as ordinary shortest-path relaxations unless the question specifically asks control traffic.
''','Links AB=1,BC=2,AC=6. Compute A’s route to C from the topology.',r'Graph knowledge::::A has descriptions of all three links, including BC.~Compare paths::w(A,C)=6,\quad w(A,B,C)=1+2=3::Use shortest-path computation.~Install forwarding::::Destination C gets next hop B and path cost 3.', 'Next hop B, total cost 3.','The installed next-hop field is B, not the full route string A,B,C.')
T('9-ipv4','cidr','IPv4;CIDR Notation','IPv4 prefixes, masks and aligned subnets',r'''
IPv4 addresses have 32 bits. A /p CIDR prefix fixes p leading bits and leaves 32−p host bits, giving 2^(32−p) total addresses. Bitwise AND of address and mask produces the prefix/network address. Under conventional subnet semantics, all-zero host bits name the network and all-one host bits the directed broadcast; /31 point-to-point and /32 cases have special conventions.
Subnet boundaries must align with host-bit blocks. A /26 has 64-address blocks, not arbitrary sets of 64 consecutive addresses starting anywhere. Aggregating two /p blocks into /p−1 requires aligned sibling prefixes sharing all earlier bits; numerical adjacency alone can cover extra addresses.
Distinguish total from conventionally usable host counts and interpret what the question asks. CIDR does not rely on old classful A/B/C default masks unless that historical model is explicitly given. Convert only the relevant bits/octets carefully, and verify that supplied host address lies between the derived network and broadcast boundaries.
''','Find the /27 network containing 192.168.4.77.',r'Block size::2^{32-27}=32::Last-octet starts are multiples of 32.~Containing range::::77 lies in 64…95, so network is 192.168.4.64.~Conventional endpoints::::Broadcast is .95, and usable host range is .65…94.', 'Network 192.168.4.64/27, with 32 total and 30 conventional host addresses.','77 AND 224 equals 64 in the last octet, independently confirming the mask result.')
T('9-ipv4','nat','Network Address Translation','NAT mappings, ports and inbound traffic',r'''
Network address translation rewrites address fields as traffic crosses a boundary. A common NAPT/PAT arrangement maps many private internal endpoints to one public address using distinct transport ports. The translator keeps state linking an internal address/port/protocol to a public endpoint and often the remote endpoint, depending on mapping behavior.
On outbound traffic, it rewrites source address/port and updates relevant checksums. Returning traffic matching its state is translated to the internal destination. Unsolicited inbound traffic needs a suitable existing mapping, static forwarding or other policy; knowing the public address alone does not identify which private host should receive it.
NAT changes address transparency and can complicate protocols embedding addresses in payloads or expecting inbound connectivity. It does not itself prove confidentiality or guarantee every application works. Private address ranges and port translation are different concepts. GATE table questions should track protocol as well as ports, since TCP and UDP numbering are distinct and several clients can reuse a private local port.
''','Two clients 10.0.0.2:5000 and 10.0.0.3:5000 contact the same server through one public IP.',r'Allocate mappings::::Give the public flows distinct translated source ports, say 40001 and 40002.~Outbound rewrite::::Both use the public source IP but retain distinct mapped endpoints.~Return demultiplexing::::A reply to 40002 maps back to 10.0.0.3:5000.', 'Port translation distinguishes the two internal sessions.','The internal source ports being equal does not require the translated ports to be equal.')
T('9-ipv4','prefix','Longest prefix','Longest-prefix forwarding and route selection',r'''
A destination can match several installed routing prefixes, including a default /0. Forwarding chooses the longest matching prefix, because it describes the most specific region. A /24 matching route therefore outranks a /16 matching route for that destination, regardless of the table’s listing order.
First check actual membership in each prefix using mask comparisons, then compare lengths among matches. A longer prefix that does not contain the destination is irrelevant. Metrics and administrative selection typically choose among routes to the same prefix before the forwarding table is used; they do not replace longest-prefix specificity in the basic lookup model.
A default handles destinations lacking more specific matches, not all traffic simply because it appears first. A /32 host route can override a containing network route. GATE questions often supply overlapping prefixes whose decimal addresses appear similar; check the complete significant prefix bits. Next-hop addresses are the outputs of the selected entries, not alternative destination-prefix candidates.
''','Routes /0→R0, 10.1.0.0/16→R1, 10.1.2.0/24→R2. Forward to 10.1.2.9.',r'Membership::::All three prefixes contain this destination.~Specificity::24>16>0::The /24 is the longest match.~Forward::::Use R2 as the next hop according to its installed entry.', 'Next hop R2.','For 10.1.3.9 the /24 would fail membership, so R1 would win instead.')
T('9-fragmentation','fragments','IPv4 Fragmentation;MTU;Fragment offsets','IPv4 fragmentation and eight-byte offsets',r'''
MTU limits the IP packet size carried by the link under this model, including its IP header. Fragmentation splits the original payload and creates a header for each fragment. The offset records each fragment’s position in the original payload in units of eight bytes, not its transmitted position among repeated headers. Identification and endpoint/protocol information associate fragments for reassembly.
With header H and MTU M, nonfinal payload capacity is 8 floor((M−H)/8). Nonfinal data must be eight-byte aligned so the next fragment position is representable; the final remainder need not be. MF is one when more original payload follows, zero only on the final piece. Fragment total length adds its own header back.
Validate by summing payloads to the original payload, keeping every total length within MTU and checking offsets against cumulative data. A DF restriction can prevent ordinary IPv4 router fragmentation. If a fragment is fragmented again, offsets remain relative to the original payload and MF reflects original continuation. IPv6 routers do not perform the same IPv4 in-network fragmentation behavior.
''','A 2020-byte IPv4 datagram with 20-byte header crosses MTU 1020.',r'Payload and capacity::P=2020-20=2000,\quad D=8\lfloor1000/8\rfloor=1000::Both nonfinal payload and remainder fit.~Pieces::::Payloads are 1000,1000; total lengths 1020,1020.~Offsets and flags::offsets=(0,125),\quad MF=(1,0)::The second begins after 1000 original data bytes.', 'Two fragments, offsets 0 and 125.','Payload sum is 2000, while transmitted totals sum to 2040 because there are two headers.')
T('9-tcp-web','flow','TCP flow control','TCP bytes, ACKs and receiver flow control',r'''
TCP provides an ordered byte stream, using sequence numbers to identify byte positions. A segment with sequence s and b data bytes covers s through s+b−1. A cumulative ACK normally names the next byte expected, not the last byte received; out-of-order data beyond a gap need not advance it. SYN and FIN each consume sequence space under the protocol.
Flow control protects receiver buffering through an advertised receive window rwnd. New data must fit the permitted outstanding region, subject also to sender congestion window cwnd. Thus use min(rwnd,cwnd) and subtract bytes already outstanding; window size is not automatically additional sending allowance.
Retransmitted data reuses its original byte numbering rather than becoming a new application message. TCP segmentation boundaries do not preserve application writes. In GATE, determine whether the question asks next ACK, total in-flight limit or unused sending allowance. Preserve bytes versus bits when combining a window with RTT or bandwidth, and note whether control flags are included in sequence arithmetic.
''','SEQ=1000, payload 200 bytes, no gaps or control flags. Then window limit 1000 with 600 outstanding.',r'Covered interval::::Bytes are 1000…1199 inclusive.~Acknowledgment::ACK=1000+200=1200::This is the next expected byte.~New allowance::1000-600=400\ bytes::Outstanding data already occupies the window.', 'ACK 1200; new sending allowance 400 bytes.','An ACK of 1199 would name the last delivered byte rather than TCP’s next-expected convention.')
T('9-tcp-web','congestion','TCP congestion control','Congestion window, slow start and loss response',r'''
Congestion control limits sender load on the network through cwnd, separate from receiver capacity rwnd. Classic slow start increases cwnd roughly exponentially per RTT while acknowledgments arrive, then congestion avoidance grows more slowly under the specified variant. A threshold determines the transition. Timeout and duplicate-ACK recovery can trigger different reductions and retransmission behavior.
Exact rules depend on the TCP variant and acknowledgment model. Reno-style additive increase/multiplicative decrease is a common exercise, but modern congestion controllers may behave differently. A loss is evidence used by the algorithm, not proof that receiver memory is full. Flow control can constrain sending even with a large cwnd, and congestion control can constrain it with a large receive window.
Trace cwnd at well-defined RTT boundaries and specify MSS/byte units. Per-ACK increments and delayed acknowledgments can alter simplified growth assumptions. A window/RTT estimate is an upper bound also limited by link bandwidth, startup and loss. GATE problems supplying an algorithm’s exact recurrence should use those rules rather than an unsupported universal “TCP doubles forever.”
''','Simplified slow start begins at 1 MSS and doubles each complete RTT until reaching 8 MSS.',r'First RTT::cwnd=2\ MSS::All modeled acknowledgments arrive without loss.~Second RTT::cwnd=4\ MSS::Apply the same growth rule.~Third RTT::cwnd=8\ MSS::The stated threshold is reached; later rules must be specified separately.', 'After three modeled RTTs cwnd reaches 8 MSS.','Receiver window can still cap actual in-flight data below cwnd; these values do not alone determine throughput.')
T('9-sockets','connection','Socket API;bind;listen;accept;connect','Socket endpoints and TCP connection establishment',r'''
A socket is a communication endpoint exposed through an API, identified using protocol and local/remote addresses and ports as appropriate. A typical TCP server creates a socket, binds a local address/port, listens, then accepts connections. Accept returns a connected socket while the listening socket remains available for further clients. A client uses connect to establish a peer relationship.
Many accepted connections can share the server’s local port because remote endpoints distinguish flows. TCP and UDP have separate protocol namespaces, so equal numeric port values do not necessarily conflict across protocols. Binding policies and reuse flags are OS-specific details that require the stated model.
A connected socket is not interchangeable with the listener for application I/O. Connection establishment can fail or be asynchronous under the selected mode. Calls to accept/connect and the underlying packet handshake are related but not identical conceptual events. In GATE traces, label listener, connected socket and remote client separately; one server port does not imply the server can handle only one simultaneous connection.
''','A server listens on port 80 and accepts two clients with different source endpoints.',r'Listener::::The original socket stays in listening role.~First accept::::A new connected socket represents the first client/server endpoint pair.~Second accept::::Another connected socket has the same local port but a different remote endpoint.', 'One listener and two connected sockets can coexist.','A flow’s remote address/port helps distinguish connections sharing the server port.')
T('9-sockets','stream','send;recv','Partial send/recv and application message framing',r'''
TCP preserves bytes in order but not application call boundaries. One send can be received by several recv calls, or several sends can be combined in one read. A positive short return is the amount completed, not evidence that an entire intended message has arrived. A zero-byte stream read conventionally indicates orderly end, unlike a short positive read.
Applications define framing using a length prefix, delimiter or fixed size. With a length prefix, accumulate the complete header, validate its declared length, then collect exactly that payload size; retain extra received bytes for the next message. Send loops continue from the first unsent byte rather than resending the entire buffer after partial progress.
Handle errors, closure and nonblocking would-block states according to the API. A buffer capacity limits one read call, not the network message length. UDP exposes datagram boundaries under its API but has different delivery guarantees. GATE questions about “one send equals one recv” test stream semantics, so answer from those guarantees rather than an observation on one fast local run.
''','A message has 8 payload bytes. The first recv returns 3.',r'Accumulate::::Store the three received bytes as the message prefix.~Remaining amount::8-3=5::Five payload bytes are still needed.~Continue::::Receive and append until complete or a meaningful closure/error occurs.', 'A short read is not by itself a protocol failure or message completion.','Calling recv again must append after the existing three bytes, not overwrite them.')
T('9-dns','resolution','DNS;Recursive resolution;Iterative resolution','DNS hierarchy, referrals and recursive service',r'''
DNS maps names to typed resource records in a delegated hierarchy. A client stub often asks a recursive resolver to obtain the final answer. That resolver may query root, top-level-domain and authoritative servers as needed, receiving referrals or answers. In an iterative exchange, a server supplies what it knows and the requester follows the next referral.
Recursive versus iterative describes who performs the remaining resolution work; it is not simply whether “many packets” are used. Cached answers and delegation records can skip levels, so every lookup need not contact root. An authoritative answer comes from the responsible zone, while a cached response can still be valid without that server contact.
DNS commonly uses UDP but also TCP and other transports, so not every response is one small UDP packet. A CNAME target may require additional resolution. GATE delay counting should identify cache state, serial query exchanges and any parallel operations. A referral to another nameserver is not automatically the requested host’s numeric address.
''','An uncached name example.com is resolved through a recursive resolver.',r'Client request::::The stub asks the resolver for the result.~Resolver work::::The resolver follows necessary root/TLD referrals to the domain’s authoritative service.~Return::::It supplies the answer to the stub and caches eligible records.', 'The stub delegates the work; iterative exchanges can occur within the resolver’s process.','An existing valid cache entry can bypass some or all of those network exchanges.')
T('9-dns','records','TTL;Resource records','DNS record types, aliases and cache lifetime',r'''
Resource records specify a name, type, class, TTL and type-dependent data. A holds an IPv4 address, AAAA an IPv6 address, NS names an authoritative/delegated server, MX names a mail exchanger with preference, and CNAME makes an alias to another name. The record type determines what the data means; an MX name is not already its numeric IP address.
TTL bounds how long a cached record can be reused under ordinary expiry handling. Remaining lifetime decreases with elapsed time and is not renewed simply because a client reads it. An authoritative new response can provide fresh information. Different cached records in one resolution path can have different lifetimes.
Negative caching and additional-section data follow protocol rules rather than a blanket “cache everything forever.” For GATE arithmetic, state no refresh/eviction and subtract age from TTL, not from wall-clock midnight. For alias traces, follow the target until the requested record is found and avoid treating an alias label as the address. DNS naming does not itself establish a transport connection to the resulting service.
''','An A record with TTL 180 seconds was cached 65 seconds ago; no refresh occurred.',r'Age::::65 seconds of validity have elapsed.~Remaining time::180-65=115\ seconds::The stored lifetime is reduced, not reset.~Expiry behavior::::After the remaining period, fresh lookup information is needed before ordinary reuse.', '115 seconds of remaining cache validity.','Repeated client queries during this interval do not independently renew the original cached TTL.')
T('9-http','semantics','HTTP;Methods;Status codes','HTTP request semantics and response status',r'''
HTTP is an application request/response protocol describing resources and representations. A request uses a method, target and headers, with an optional body; the response supplies a status, headers and optional representation. GET retrieves, HEAD asks for corresponding metadata without a response body, POST submits processing content, PUT replaces/creates under its semantics and DELETE requests removal.
Safe methods do not request a state-changing action; idempotent methods have the same intended effect when repeated, which differs from identical response bytes. GET is conventionally safe/idempotent, PUT and DELETE idempotent but not safe, and POST is not generally idempotent. Actual faulty application implementations can violate intended method semantics.
Status categories include success 2xx, redirection 3xx, client-related 4xx and server-related 5xx. 404 is a valid HTTP response indicating a missing target under response semantics, not transport connection refusal. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. Read the version and security/setup assumptions before applying a latency model.
''','A request returns HTTP 404. Did the TCP connection necessarily fail?',r'Protocol evidence::::Receiving an HTTP response means an application exchange occurred over the transport.~Status meaning::::404 describes the requested resource outcome, not failure to establish a connection.~Contrast::::A connection refusal can prevent any HTTP status response from existing.', '404 is an HTTP error response, not synonymous with connection refusal.','A browser can display both as failures, but their protocol stage and troubleshooting are different.')
T('9-http','reuse','Persistence;Caching','Persistent connections, multiplexing and cache validation',r'''
Persistence reuses a connection for several requests, avoiding repeated setup. HTTP/1.1 without pipelining can still issue requests sequentially; HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over one connection; HTTP/3 uses QUIC stream mechanisms. Reuse does not imply all object transfers complete simultaneously or that bottleneck bandwidth disappears.
A fresh cache response can avoid contacting the origin. A stale cached representation may be conditionally validated using a validator; a 304 response confirms reuse without transmitting the complete representation body, but the network exchange still costs time. Cache policy, freshness and validation are separate choices.
A simplified new-TCP single-object model counts one RTT for setup, one for request/response start and transmission, omitting DNS/TLS unless specified. Existing connections remove the setup RTT. Multi-object time depends on ordering, parallel connections, multiplexing and transfer overlap. GATE calculations should construct a timeline, not multiply one object’s time by count while also claiming simultaneous requests.
''','Three sequential small requests use one persistent established connection; each exchange costs one RTT, ignore transfers.',r'Setup assumption::::The connection already exists, so no setup RTT is counted.~Sequential exchanges::::Requests cannot overlap under the stated model.~Total::T=3RTT::One RTT per exchange adds.', '3 RTTs under this model.','A fresh connection would add the specified setup cost; parallel/multiplexed exchanges would need a different timeline.')
